import 'career_handbook_entry.dart';

/// One combined narrative entry per browsable vertical (see
/// `kAllBrowsableVerticals` in `corps_affinity.dart`) — verticalName must
/// match a real `CareerVertical.name` exactly for every entry. Combines two
/// independently AI-drafted handbook documents: certifications and job
/// titles are never restated here (they come only from `career_vertical.dart`)
/// — this file is narrative context only, reviewed for fabrication before use.
const List<CareerHandbookEntry> kCareerHandbookEntries = [
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Operations & Process Excellence',
    whatItDoes: 'Operations keeps a company\'s core product or service moving reliably from one step to the next — the shop floor, a fulfillment hub, a back-office processing centre — while controlling quality, cost and waste. It sets operating standards, tracks throughput and resource use, and fixes recurring problems before they become customer-facing failures. This matters because even a strong product can lose money or customers if day-to-day execution is slow, inconsistent or wasteful.',
    careerRoadmap: 'An officer typically starts by running a single defined operation or process, watching output, quality, manpower and cost directly. Responsibility then widens to multiple teams, shifts or a larger facility with cross-location performance comparisons and improvement projects, then to a major operating area or business unit where the focus shifts to setting standards and managing other managers. At the top of the ladder the scope becomes enterprise-wide, connecting operating performance to strategy, resource allocation and long-term resilience.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Execution-biased, resilient leaders who are comfortable in high-tempo, metrics-driven environments',
      'Combat arms and logistics officers tend to transition especially well, given their comfort commanding large teams under pressure',
      'People who can balance empathy for a frontline workforce with a hard focus on numbers',
      'A preference for measurable results and process discipline',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Dislike of daily firefighting or a preference for slow-paced strategic thinking over immediate action',
      'Discomfort with spreadsheet-based data analysis and close attention to operating detail',
      'Needing formal command authority rather than influence to get things done',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree is generally acceptable, though technical or operations-related education helps. Six Sigma Green/Black Belt and PMP are the recognised bridge certifications — the former for process-improvement credibility, the latter for structured delivery. A CV should quantify the scale of teams, budgets or multi-site activity managed and any measurable efficiency gains, since no professional licence is normally required but civilian employers need proof that military command experience converts into operating results.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Supply Chain & Procurement',
    whatItDoes: 'This function makes sure a company has the materials, equipment and services it needs, when it needs them, at an acceptable cost — spanning sourcing, procurement, warehousing and logistics. It protects margins by keeping inventory lean and sourcing costs controlled, while preventing the production delays and stockouts that come from weak supplier management. The work is as much about negotiation and risk management as it is about tracking shipments.',
    careerRoadmap: 'The typical path starts with running a defined node of the chain — a regional warehouse or a specific procurement category — before moving to coordinating multiple vendors, categories or sites and negotiating within set limits. Later tiers own a major supply-chain area end-to-end, including supplier strategy and continuity risk, before scaling to regional or enterprise-wide network design and, at the top, shaping working capital, resilience and supplier strategy across the whole organisation.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Highly analytical, negotiation-savvy officers who are comfortable working with numbers and multiple stakeholders',
      'Army Ordnance Corps, Army Service Corps, Navy Logistics and Air Force Logistics officers transition exceptionally well here',
      'Comfort squeezing value out of large contracts and anticipating cascading effects of delays',
      'Willingness to translate military logistics experience into commercial, supplier-facing terms',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Discomfort with intense or adversarial negotiation',
      'A preference for leading people directly over managing data streams and vendor relationships',
      'Dislike of detailed tracking and the constant trade-off between cost, speed and resilience',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree can work, though engineering, business or supply-chain education is helpful. CSCP (APICS) and CPSM are the certifications that carry the most weight, proving familiarity with modern commercial supply-chain frameworks. A CV should highlight the financial value of inventory or assets managed and the scale of the logistics networks controlled; there is no universal professional licence required, but demonstrated commercial thinking matters more than military logistics terminology alone.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Security, Risk & Crisis Management',
    whatItDoes: 'This function protects a company\'s people, facilities, information and operational continuity from threats ranging from theft and violence to fraud and natural disasters. It combines prevention, preparedness, incident response and recovery, connecting directly to the business by preventing financial loss and helping the company survive disruption with minimal downtime. Done well, it enables the business to operate rather than locking it down.',
    careerRoadmap: 'Officers typically begin running security arrangements for a single site or defined operation — guards, access control, incident response, local risk assessment — then move to coordinating security across several sites or a region, setting standards and handling more complex incidents. Later tiers own the corporate security programme, threat assessments and crisis plans for an entire business, then integrate physical security with crisis management and enterprise resilience at the executive level, ultimately advising senior leadership on the organisation\'s overall security posture.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Calm, observant officers who can assess threats without overreacting and communicate clearly under pressure',
      'Combat arms, Provost and Intelligence officers are a natural fit, though the field is not limited to a particular arm or service',
      'People who understand security as a service function that must enable the business rather than obstruct it',
      'Comfort building relationships with local law enforcement and external stakeholders',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'A rigid \'fortress\' mentality that resists balancing security against business needs',
      'Difficulty justifying return on investment for security spend',
      'Preference for predictable desk work over incident response and stakeholder negotiation',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree is generally acceptable. CPP (ASIS) is the certification most likely to be recognised, alongside NEBOSH for safety and Business Continuity Institute (BCI) certification for continuity planning. A CV should showcase the complexity of security operations managed, specific incidents mitigated, and experience with modern electronic security systems; being able to demonstrate a clean background and physical fitness is often expected, though requirements vary by industry.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Integrated Facilities Management',
    whatItDoes: 'This function keeps corporate real estate — offices, campuses, labs — functioning so employees can work safely and productively. It covers real estate leasing and space planning, maintenance, housekeeping, cafeteria services and workplace safety, and manages a significant share of operating expenditure. Beyond upkeep, it directly affects employee productivity, retention and corporate culture.',
    careerRoadmap: 'The path usually starts with running the day-to-day operations of a single building or small campus — vendors, maintenance, safety — before expanding to larger campuses or multiple regional offices with bigger contracts and space-utilisation tracking. Mid-career tiers own workplace and property strategy across a portfolio, negotiating leases and major fit-out projects, before scaling to enterprise-wide real estate and infrastructure strategy and, ultimately, aligning the company\'s physical footprint with its long-term strategic vision.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Service-oriented, highly organised and financially literate officers',
      'Engineers (particularly Civil or Electrical), Quartermasters, and those with extensive Cantonment Board or Station HQ experience are strong fits',
      'A hospitality mindset paired with engineering common sense',
      'Comfort managing contractors, budgets and competing user demands',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Viewing administrative support roles as beneath the role',
      'Impatience with daily user complaints or vendor management',
      'Preference for work that is visible rather than judged by whether problems quietly disappear',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'An engineering degree or general graduation is generally acceptable. IFMA CFM and RICS are the listed bridge certifications, useful for establishing civilian credibility in facilities and property. A CV should highlight the square footage, budgets or service contracts managed and familiarity with health, safety and environment standards; formal registration is not usually a hard prerequisite for management roles, though some technical work may require licensed specialists.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Aviation, Maritime & Fleet Management',
    whatItDoes: 'This vertical manages a company\'s large moving assets — an airline\'s flight and ground operations, a shipping line\'s port operations, or a national vehicle fleet. It exists to maximise the utilisation of expensive, time-sensitive assets while meeting strict safety and regulatory standards, since delays or incidents quickly affect customers, revenue and reputation.',
    careerRoadmap: 'Officers typically start by running the daily turnaround of assets at a specific hub — dispatch, loading, scheduling and maintenance. They then move to overseeing multiple hubs or a larger fleet, handling disruptions and liaising with regulators, before directing operations for a major division with accountability for efficiency and safety-incident outcomes. At the senior tiers the role shifts to setting network strategy, fleet investment decisions and, ultimately, global asset and logistics strategy.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Process-obsessed, safety-first leaders who grasp the financial cost of asset downtime',
      'Air Force pilots and ground-duty officers, Navy executive-branch officers and Army Service Corps officers are direct fits',
      'Comfort operating in heavily unionised, tightly regulated environments',
      'Sector-specific domain background, since this vertical is usually not accessible without one',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Difficulty working within strict regulatory frameworks',
      'No prior exposure to complex rostering or asset-yield thinking',
      'Wanting a fast-changing environment rather than one governed by fixed procedure',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Specific domain experience is usually expected — aviation operations roles, for example, generally require an aviation background. IATA Airport Operations certification and Certificate in Maritime Operations are the bridge tools most useful for translating military experience into commercial terms. A CV should highlight the scale of fleets or hubs managed and safety outcomes; some regulated technical or command positions may require specific operating licences that should not be assumed from military experience alone.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'BFSI & Financial Crime Risk',
    whatItDoes: 'Within banking, financial services and insurance, this function prevents the institution from being used for money laundering, terrorism financing or fraud, protecting it from regulatory penalties and reputational damage. The work is evidence-heavy — reviewing transactions and customers, setting controls, investigating alerts and escalating significant risk — while trying not to slow down legitimate customer onboarding.',
    careerRoadmap: 'The typical progression begins with managing analysts who conduct customer checks and investigate transaction alerts against strict accuracy and turnaround targets. It moves to running larger teams handling more complex cases and regulatory reporting, then to owning a major financial-crime operation and its control framework, including facing regulators during audits. At the top tiers, financial-crime risk is integrated into a broader enterprise risk view, culminating in ultimate accountability for the organisation\'s overall risk framework.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Detail-obsessed, highly analytical, process-driven officers',
      'Intelligence officers, financial specialists and Provost officers with investigation backgrounds tend to fit well',
      'Comfort digesting complex regulatory text and turning it into operational workflows',
      'Patience for documentation and repetitive case review',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Dislike of desk-bound data analysis or repetitive compliance work',
      'Preference for physical action over digital investigation',
      'Discomfort making decisions that must be justified from evidence rather than instinct',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree can be a starting point, though finance or accounting knowledge helps. CAMS is close to a hard prerequisite for being taken seriously in financial-crime work, with FRM more relevant as the role broadens into wider financial risk. A CV should demonstrate management of analytical teams, understanding of risk typologies, and experience with strict compliance frameworks; regulatory permissions vary by role and there is no single universal licence for this path.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Intelligence & Vigilance',
    whatItDoes: 'This vertical acts as a company\'s internal early-warning and integrity function, looking for misconduct, conflicts of interest, corruption and other threats to organisational integrity, alongside gathering external information on competitors and market risks. It protects the company\'s integrity and gives decision-makers an information advantage, working through careful, fair fact-finding rather than confrontation.',
    careerRoadmap: 'Officers typically begin by executing background checks and investigating lower-level complaints while maintaining day-to-day information-gathering networks. The role then grows to managing more complex investigations involving middle management and identifying systemic vulnerabilities, before overseeing the entire vigilance and intelligence apparatus, including highly sensitive investigations involving senior executives. At the top tier, the officer holds independent, board-facing accountability for the organisation\'s overall integrity and vigilance framework.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Discreet, highly ethical officers with a healthy dose of professional scepticism',
      'Military Intelligence, Provost and officers with deep investigative or specialised operational backgrounds tend to thrive',
      'Comfort operating in the grey areas of corporate politics while maintaining personal integrity',
      'Ability to write meticulous, legal-grade reports',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Lack of tact or discomfort with confidentiality',
      'Difficulty with the isolation that can come from investigating colleagues',
      'Situations where personal relationships must not influence judgement',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree is generally acceptable. Certified Fraud Examiner (CFE) is a key bridge certification for understanding commercial fraud typologies, alongside a Certificate in Corporate Vigilance. A CV should highlight experience managing sensitive information networks, conducting interviews, and presenting intelligence briefs to senior leadership; credibility here depends heavily on demonstrating impartial, evidence-based casework.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Corporate Investigations',
    whatItDoes: 'When something goes wrong — embezzlement, harassment, data theft, vendor kickbacks — this function establishes exactly what happened and preserves the evidence needed for defensible legal or disciplinary action. Unlike broader vigilance work, it is a reactive, highly specialised function built on interviews, record analysis and evidence handling that can end up in court or before regulators.',
    careerRoadmap: 'The typical path starts with conducting witness interviews, analysing records for anomalies and drafting investigation reports on routine cases. It progresses to leading complex, multi-disciplinary investigations and coordinating with digital forensics and external agencies, then to owning the entire investigations docket for a region or company, including managing the legal risk of investigations themselves. At the senior tiers, the role builds enterprise forensic capability and, ultimately, provides assurance directly to the board on the most serious cases.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Skeptical, detail-oriented and highly articulate officers comfortable with confrontation',
      'Military Police, Intelligence and JAG officers tend to be strong fits, since legal training bridges naturally into evidentiary procedure',
      'A strong stomach for dealing with emotional or deceptive subjects while remaining objective',
      'Patience for meticulous documentation and lengthy evidence review',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Being trusting to a fault',
      'Difficulty with meticulous, sustained documentation',
      'Impatience with sifting through large volumes of records to find a single piece of evidence',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree works, though a background in law or accounting is an advantage. Certified Fraud Examiner (CFE) is close to essential, supported by a Certified Forensic Investigation Professional credential. A CV should demonstrate a track record of closing complex cases, understanding of the laws of evidence, and the ability to run discreet, high-stakes investigations.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Brand Protection & IPR',
    whatItDoes: 'This vertical hunts down counterfeiters, trademark infringers and parallel importers who steal a company\'s intellectual property. It protects revenue and brand reputation by getting fake or unauthorised products — which can be unsafe or poor quality — off the market, both physically and online, often working with law enforcement and customs authorities.',
    careerRoadmap: 'Officers typically start by handling reported infringements directly — monitoring for fake listings, coordinating market sweeps and enforcement raids. The role then grows to mapping entire illicit supply chains and managing investigations into clandestine manufacturing, before owning regional enforcement strategy, external law-firm relationships and prioritisation of the highest-value threats. At the top tiers, the officer aligns IP protection with overall business strategy and coordinates worldwide enforcement efforts to protect the company\'s most valuable intangible assets.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Tenacious, legally minded, street-smart operators',
      'Officers with counter-insurgency, intelligence-networking or complex logistics-tracking experience do well, since hunting counterfeiters mirrors mapping illicit networks',
      'Comfort working closely with law enforcement and external agencies',
      'A working grasp of basic trademark and IP concepts',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'Wanting a purely desk-bound role',
      'Reluctance to work closely with law enforcement',
      'Difficulty grasping legal and commercial complexity in enforcement decisions',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree works, and a law degree is a considerable bonus. Certified Fraud Examiner (CFE) and a foundational IP/Brand Protection fundamentals certificate are useful bridges. A CV should show the ability to manage external agencies, conduct network analysis to trace a problem to its source, and run operations alongside local authorities.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Project & Program Management (PMO)',
    whatItDoes: 'The Project Management Office ensures a company\'s strategic initiatives actually get executed — building a new factory, launching a new IT system, integrating an acquisition. While operations runs the daily business, the PMO manages unique, temporary endeavours, keeping complex projects on time, on budget and within scope as connective tissue between corporate silos.',
    careerRoadmap: 'The path typically starts with managing a specific project with defined boundaries — schedules, status tracking, escalations. It grows to managing a \'programme\' of related projects, handling dependencies and resourcing conflicts between teams, then to running project delivery for the whole organisation, setting methodology and giving leadership visibility into progress and risk. At the top tiers, the role manages an enterprise-wide portfolio of investment and, ultimately, leads major organisational transformation.',
    whoSucceeds: [
      'Highly structured, diplomatic and relentless drivers of outcomes',
      'Officers who have managed complex acquisitions, large deployments or multi-agency exercises tend to excel',
      'Comfort leading through influence rather than direct command authority',
      'Tolerance for meticulous documentation and constant negotiation',
    ],
    whoStruggles: [
      'A need for direct command authority to get things done',
      'Dislike of meticulous documentation',
      'Difficulty coordinating many stakeholders who do not report to you',
    ],
    whatYoudNeed: 'Any graduate degree is generally acceptable. PMP is the most widely recognised bridge certification for project delivery, with PRINCE2 and Certified Scrum Master also relevant depending on methodology. A CV should quantify the budgets, timelines and cross-functional teams managed to deliver specific, defined outcomes.',
  ),
  CareerHandbookEntry(
    verticalName: 'Strategy, Consulting & Chief of Staff',
    whatItDoes: 'This vertical acts as the analytical brain trust for senior leadership, examining market trends, competitor moves and internal capability to help decide where the company should go next, and then helping ensure those priorities are actually executed. It matters because leadership time is scarce and poor strategic choices can be very costly.',
    careerRoadmap: 'Officers typically start as the analytical workhorse — building models, conducting market research and preparing materials that outline strategic options. The role then grows to owning strategic workstreams or acting as a trusted coordinator for a business leader, translating strategy into operational plans. Senior tiers run major strategic reviews and enterprise-wide initiatives close to the CEO, before culminating in evaluating major corporate choices and, at the most senior level, advising boards on strategy and governance.',
    whoSucceeds: [
